Cap the sideways part only where the footing is friction, and keep it whole under own recoil or bolts (brief 5d). In weakGround add a flag:
function weakGround(ctx, body, J, th, T, weight, friction) {
  if (!ctx.unbreakable || !isFinite(J) || !(J > 0) || !(T > 0)) return { J };
  const Nmax = qultFor(ctx.surface, body.footB) * 2 * body.footArea, cap = Math.max(0, Nmax - weight) * T;
  const Jd = J * Math.max(0, Math.sin(th)), Jh = Math.abs(Math.cos(th)) < 1e-9 ? 0 : J * Math.cos(th);
  const capH = friction ? ctx.surface.mu * Nmax * T : Infinity, Jdg = Math.min(Jd, cap), Jhg = Math.min(Jh, capH);
  if (Jdg === Jd && Jhg === Jh) return { J };
  return { J: Math.hypot(Jhg, Jdg), whole: J, supplied: (Jd - Jdg) + (Jh - Jhg), suppliedSide: Jh - Jhg };
}
ThrowOnce passes ownAnchor(ctx) === 'friction'; the catches pass ctxC.anchor === 'friction'.
- suppliedText: when suppliedSide > 0, say that their power gives the grip beyond what the ground's friction can (mu x its bearing), not that 'the ground takes the sideways part'.
- README and decision 22: the sideways part goes in whole when he takes his own recoil or is bolted (5d); on friction alone the ground gives at most mu x its bearing over the pulse.
- powers.js check: with ownRecoil off and Unbreakable on, a 45 deg car throw on sand has toGround J <= mu x Nmax x T.

